    "text": "A refrigerator works by moving heat from inside to outside, like carrying warmth away in a bucket. A special liquid called refrigerant flows through pipes. First, it passes through a part inside the fridge and absorbs heat, turning into a gas. Then a compressor squeezes that gas, making it hot. Next, the hot gas travels through coils on the back or bottom, where it releases heat into your kitchen and turns back into a liquid. Finally, it flows back inside to soak up more heat. This cycle repeats over and over, keeping the inside cold so your food stays fresh."
